    <li><span>02</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s2">Set the Azure crede=
ntials</a></li>
    <li><span>03</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s3">Write the usage log=
ger</a></li>
    <li><span>04</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s4">Write the proxy con=
fig</a></li>
    <li><span>05</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s5">Start the proxy</a>=
</li>
    <li><span>06</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s6">Smoke-test before t=
ouching the agent</a></li>
    <li><span>07</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s7">Point Codex CLI at =
the proxy</a></li>
    <li><span>08</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s8">Label runs so total=
s mean something</a></li>
    <li><span>09</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s9">Roll the log up int=
o a report</a></li>
    <li><span>10</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be038a64c=
b5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#s10">Run a comparison t=
hat=E2=80=99s actually fair</a></li>
    <li><span>=E2=80=94</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be=
038a64cb5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#trouble">Trouble=
shooting</a></li>
    <li><span>=E2=80=94</span><a href=3D"https://aac15f95-9383-4bcc-a59d-be=
038a64cb5a.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1787851747-ab71/#later">If we out=
grow the JSONL file</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s0">
  <div class=3D"step-num">WHY</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>What we=E2=80=99re actually measuring</h2>
    <p>The number a coding agent shows in its own status line is not compar=
able across agents =E2=80=94 each one counts differently, and some don=E2=
=80=99t count reasoning tokens at all. The only trustworthy source is the <=
code>usage</code> object Azure returns on each API response, which is exact=
ly what a proxy sits in a position to capture.</p>
    <p>Two things make this worth the setup effort rather than eyeballing a=
 console:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens</strong> but=
 are never shown to the user. A three-line answer can carry tens of thousan=
ds of them. Any comparison that ignores them is wrong in the direction that=
 matters for cost.</li>
      <li><strong>One task is many requests.</strong> An agent fixing a bug=
 will inspect, read files, plan, patch, run tests, and correct =E2=80=94 ea=
ch a separate call. The meaningful unit is the whole task, not a single req=
uest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So the goal is a per-request log we can total per task, broken out i=
nto the columns that drive cost:</p>
    <div class=3D"tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Why it=E2=80=99s separate</th></tr></t=
head>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>input_tokens</code></td><td>Everything sent: system=
 prompt, files, tool schemas, conversation so far.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cached_input_tokens</code></td><td>The slice Azure =
served from prompt cache =E2=80=94 billed at a discount.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>uncached_input_tokens</code></td><td>Input minus ca=
ched. This is the real input cost driver.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>output_tokens</code></td><td>Everything generated, =
reasoning included.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>reasoning_tokens</code></td><td>Hidden thinking. Us=
ually the largest surprise in an agent workload.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>visible_output_tokens</code></td><td>Output minus r=
easoning =E2=80=94 what actually reached the editor.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>request count</td><td>How chatty the agent=E2=80=99s loop=
 is. Varies more between agents than per-call size does.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s1">
  <div class=3D"step-num">01</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Install LiteLLM in a virtual environment</h2>
    <p>Do this in a dedicated folder. The proxy needs to run from the folde=
r holding the config and the logger script, because it imports the logger b=
y module name.</p>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"c"># Pick a home for this. Anywhere you like.=
</span>
mkdir <span class=3D"s">$HOME\token-metering</span>
cd <span class=3D"s">$HOME\token-metering</span>

<span class=3D"c"># Confirm Python first =E2=80=94 3.10 or newer.</span>
py --version

<span class=3D"c"># Virtual environment keeps this off your system Python.<=
/span>
py -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

pip install <span class=3D"s">"litellm[proxy]"</span>
litellm --version</code></pre>
    <div class=3D"note trap">
      <span class=3D"tag">If activation is blocked</span>
      <p>PowerShell refuses unsigned scripts by default. <code>Set-Executio=
nPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass</code> unblocks it for the c=
urrent window only, which is the least invasive fix. You=E2=80=99ll need it=
 in every new terminal, or set the scope to <code>CurrentUser</code> once.<=
/p>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"note">
      <span class=3D"tag">Every new terminal</span>
      <p>Re-run <code>.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1</code> from the project =
folder. Most =E2=80=9C<code>litellm</code> is not recognized=E2=80=9D repor=
ts are a terminal where the venv isn=E2=80=99t active.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s2">
  <div class=3D"step-num">02</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Set the Azure credentials</h2>
    <p>LiteLLM reads Azure config from <code>AZURE_*</code> variables =E2=
=80=94 not <code>OPENAI_*</code>. Set them in the window you=E2=80=99ll sta=
rt the proxy from:</p>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">$env:AZURE_API_KEY</span>     =3D <span cl=
ass=3D"s">"&lt;azure-openai-key&gt;"</span>
<span class=3D"k">$env:AZURE_API_BASE</span>    =3D <span class=3D"s">"http=
s://&lt;your-resource&gt;.openai.azure.com/"</span>
<span class=3D"k">$env:AZURE_API_VERSION</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"&lt;=
the api-version your Azure deployment uses&gt;"</span></code></pre>
    <p>Use the same endpoint and API version the agent already talks to suc=
cessfully today. Don=E2=80=99t guess an API version =E2=80=94 GPT-5.4 featu=
res get gated by it, and a version that=E2=80=99s too old will silently dro=
p <code>reasoning_effort</code> or reject tool calls.</p>
    <div class=3D"note fix">
      <span class=3D"tag">After this is working</span>
      <p>Remove the Azure key from the coding agent=E2=80=99s own config. F=
rom here on, LiteLLM is the only thing that holds the real credential; the =
agent authenticates to LiteLLM with a throwaway value.</p>
    </div>
    <p>These vanish when you close the terminal, which is the safer default=
. If you want them to persist, use <code>[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVaria=
ble("AZURE_API_KEY","&lt;key&gt;","User")</code> =E2=80=94 but be aware tha=
t writes the key to your user registry hive in plain text.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s3">
  <div class=3D"step-num">03</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Write the usage logger</h2>
    <p>This is the part that makes the exercise useful. LiteLLM lets you re=
gister a callback class that fires after every completed request; ours pull=
s the usage object apart and appends one JSON line per call. Note that it h=
andles <em>both</em> field-name conventions, because the Chat Completions a=
nd Responses APIs name the same numbers differently.</p>
    <span class=3D"filename">usage_logger.py</span>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">import</span> json, os, threading
<span class=3D"k">from</span> datetime <span class=3D"k">import</span> date=
time, timezone
<span class=3D"k">from</span> pathlib <span class=3D"k">import</span> Path
<span class=3D"k">from</span> litellm.integrations.custom_logger <span clas=
s=3D"k">import</span> CustomLogger

LOG_PATH =3D Path(os.environ.get(<span class=3D"s">"TOKEN_LOG"</span>, <spa=
n class=3D"s">"token_usage.jsonl"</span>))
_lock =3D threading.Lock()


<span class=3D"k">def</span> _d(obj):
    <span class=3D"c"># usage may arrive as a dict or as a pydantic model, =
depending on route</span>
    <span class=3D"k">if</span> obj <span class=3D"k">is</span> <span class=
=3D"k">None</span>:
        <span class=3D"k">return</span> {}
    <span class=3D"k">if</span> isinstance(obj, dict):
        <span class=3D"k">return</span> obj
    <span class=3D"k">for</span> attr <span class=3D"k">in</span> (<span cl=
ass=3D"s">"model_dump"</span>, <span class=3D"s">"dict"</span>):
        fn =3D getattr(obj, attr, <span class=3D"k">None</span>)
        <span class=3D"k">if</span> callable(fn):
            <span class=3D"k">try</span>:
                <span class=3D"k">return</span> fn()
            <span class=3D"k">except</span> Exception:
                <span class=3D"k">pass</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> getattr(obj, <span class=3D"s">"__dict_=
_"</span>, {}) <span class=3D"k">or</span> {}


<span class=3D"k">def</span> _usage(response_obj):
    u =3D _d(_d(response_obj).get(<span class=3D"s">"usage"</span>) <span c=
lass=3D"k">or</span> getattr(response_obj, <span class=3D"s">"usage"</span>=
, <span class=3D"k">None</span>))

    <span class=3D"c"># Chat Completions ....... prompt_tokens / completion=
_tokens</span>
    <span class=3D"c"># Responses API .......... input_tokens  / output_tok=
ens</span>
    inp   =3D u.get(<span class=3D"s">"prompt_tokens"</span>)     <span cla=
ss=3D"k">or</span> u.get(<span class=3D"s">"input_tokens"</span>)  <span cl=
ass=3D"k">or</span> <span class=3D"d">0</span>
    out   =3D u.get(<span class=3D"s">"completion_tokens"</span>) <span cla=
ss=3D"k">or</span> u.get(<span class=3D"s">"output_tokens"</span>) <span cl=
ass=3D"k">or</span> <span class=3D"d">0</span>
    total =3D u.get(<span class=3D"s">"total_tokens"</span>) <span class=3D=
"k">or</span> (inp + out)

    in_det  =3D _d(u.get(<span class=3D"s">"prompt_tokens_details"</span>) =
    <span class=3D"k">or</span> u.get(<span class=3D"s">"input_tokens_detai=
ls"</span>))
    out_det =3D _d(u.get(<span class=3D"s">"completion_tokens_details"</spa=
n>) <span class=3D"k">or</span> u.get(<span class=3D"s">"output_tokens_deta=
ils"</span>))

    cached    =3D in_det.get(<span class=3D"s">"cached_tokens"</span>)     =
<span class=3D"k">or</span> <span class=3D"d">0</span>
    reasoning =3D out_det.get(<span class=3D"s">"reasoning_tokens"</span>) =
<span class=3D"k">or</span> <span class=3D"d">0</span>

    <span class=3D"k">return</span> {
        <span class=3D"s">"input_tokens"</span>:           inp,
        <span class=3D"s">"cached_input_tokens"</span>:    cached,
        <span class=3D"s">"uncached_input_tokens"</span>:  max(inp - cached=
, <span class=3D"d">0</span>),
        <span class=3D"s">"output_tokens"</span>:          out,
        <span class=3D"s">"reasoning_tokens"</span>:       reasoning,
        <span class=3D"s">"visible_output_tokens"</span>:  max(out - reason=
ing, <span class=3D"d">0</span>),
        <span class=3D"s">"total_tokens"</span>:           total,
    }


<span class=3D"k">class</span> TokenUsageLogger(CustomLogger):

    <span class=3D"k">def</span> _write(self, kwargs, response_obj, start_t=
ime, end_time):
        meta    =3D (kwargs.get(<span class=3D"s">"litellm_params"</span>) =
<span class=3D"k">or</span> {}).get(<span class=3D"s">"metadata"</span>) <s=
pan class=3D"k">or</span> {}
        headers =3D meta.get(<span class=3D"s">"headers"</span>) <span clas=
s=3D"k">or</span> {}

        row =3D {
            <span class=3D"s">"ts"</span>:        datetime.now(timezone.utc=
).isoformat(),
            <span class=3D"s">"run"</span>:       headers.get(<span class=
=3D"s">"x-run-label"</span>) <span class=3D"k">or</span> os.environ.get(<sp=
an class=3D"s">"RUN_LABEL"</span>, <span class=3D"s">"unlabeled"</span>),
            <span class=3D"s">"model"</span>:     kwargs.get(<span class=3D=
"s">"model"</span>),
            <span class=3D"s">"call_type"</span>: kwargs.get(<span class=3D=
"s">"call_type"</span>),
            <span class=3D"s">"latency_s"</span>: round((end_time - start_t=
ime).total_seconds(), <span class=3D"d">3</span>)
                          <span class=3D"k">if</span> start_time <span clas=
s=3D"k">and</span> end_time <span class=3D"k">else</span> <span class=3D"k"=
>None</span>,
            **_usage(response_obj),
        }

        <span class=3D"k">with</span> _lock, LOG_PATH.open(<span class=3D"s=
">"a"</span>, encoding=3D<span class=3D"s">"utf-8"</span>) <span class=3D"k=
">as</span> f:
            f.write(json.dumps(row) + <span class=3D"s">"\n"</span>)

    <span class=3D"k">def</span> log_success_event(self, kwargs, response_o=
bj, start_time, end_time):
        <span class=3D"k">try</span>:
            self._write(kwargs, response_obj, start_time, end_time)
        <span class=3D"k">except</span> Exception <span class=3D"k">as</spa=
n> e:
            print(<span class=3D"s">f"[usage_logger] skipped a row: {e}"</s=
pan>)

    <span class=3D"k">async</span> <span class=3D"k">def</span> async_log_s=
uccess_event(self, kwargs, response_obj, start_time, end_time):
        <span class=3D"k">try</span>:
            self._write(kwargs, response_obj, start_time, end_time)
        <span class=3D"k">except</span> Exception <span class=3D"k">as</spa=
n> e:
            print(<span class=3D"s">f"[usage_logger] skipped a row: {e}"</s=
pan>)


<span class=3D"c"># config.yaml refers to this instance by name</span>
proxy_handler_instance =3D TokenUsageLogger()</code></pre>
    <div class=3D"note">
      <span class=3D"tag">On the run label</span>
      <p>The <code>x-run-label</code> header is the nicer path if your agen=
t can send custom headers; the <code>RUN_LABEL</code> environment variable =
is the reliable fallback. Step 08 covers both.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s4">
  <div class=3D"step-num">04</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Write the proxy config</h2>
    <p>Two things here are worth understanding rather than copying blindly:=
 how the Azure deployment is addressed, and why <code>base_model</code> is =
present.</p>
    <span class=3D"filename">config.yaml</span>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">model_list</span>:
  - <span class=3D"k">model_name</span>: gpt-5.4                      <span=
 class=3D"c"># what the agent asks for</span>
    <span class=3D"k">litellm_params</span>:
      <span class=3D"k">model</span>: azure/&lt;AZURE_DEPLOYMENT_NAME&gt;  =
   <span class=3D"c"># your deployment, not the model name</span>
      <span class=3D"k">api_base</span>: os.environ/AZURE_API_BASE
      <span class=3D"k">api_key</span>: os.environ/AZURE_API_KEY
      <span class=3D"k">api_version</span>: os.environ/AZURE_API_VERSION
    <span class=3D"k">model_info</span>:
      <span class=3D"k">base_model</span>: azure/gpt-5.4                <sp=
an class=3D"c"># tells LiteLLM what it really is</span>

<span class=3D"k">litellm_settings</span>:
  <span class=3D"k">callbacks</span>: usage_logger.proxy_handler_instance
  <span class=3D"k">drop_params</span>: <span class=3D"k">true</span></code=
></pre>

    <h3>The deployment name is not the model name</h3>
    <p>In Azure, <code>litellm_params.model</code> must be <code>azure/</co=
de> followed by <em>the deployment name you created in the portal</em>. If =
the deployment is called <code>coding-agent-gpt54-prod</code>, that line re=
ads <code>azure/coding-agent-gpt54-prod</code>. The friendly <code>model_na=
me: gpt-5.4</code> above it is purely what the agent sees.</p>

    <div class=3D"note trap">
      <span class=3D"tag">Known sharp edge</span>
      <p>LiteLLM decides whether to apply GPT-5.4 behaviour =E2=80=94 the R=
esponses API bridge, <code>reasoning_effort</code>, tool-call handling =E2=
=80=94 by pattern-matching the model string. A deployment name that doesn=
=E2=80=99t plainly contain <code>gpt-5.4</code> has been reported to fall t=
hrough that check and produce validation errors on tool calls. <code>model_=
info.base_model</code> is the supported way to declare the real identity, a=
nd it=E2=80=99s also what makes cost attribution correct. If you still hit =
parameter-rejection errors, the cheapest fix is to create a second Azure de=
ployment whose name contains <code>gpt-5.4</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <p><code>drop_params: true</code> tells LiteLLM to strip parameters the=
 target model doesn=E2=80=99t accept instead of erroring. The GPT-5 series =
rejects <code>max_tokens</code> in favour of <code>max_completion_tokens</c=
ode>, and agents send the old one constantly.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s5">
  <div class=3D"step-num">05</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Start the proxy</h2>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"c"># from $HOME\token-metering, with the venv=
 active</span>
litellm --config .\config.yaml --port 4000</code></pre>
    <p>Leave this window open =E2=80=94 it=E2=80=99s the running proxy. Sta=
rt the agent in a second terminal.</p>
    <div class=3D"note check">
      <span class=3D"tag">Confirm before moving on</span>
      <p>Startup output includes a line about loading the custom callback. =
If you don=E2=80=99t see the logger mentioned, the import failed =E2=80=94 =
almost always because you started <code>litellm</code> from a different fol=
der than the one holding <code>usage_logger.py</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <p>It will print that it=E2=80=99s listening on <code>0.0.0.0:4000</cod=
e>. That=E2=80=99s the bind address, meaning =E2=80=9Call interfaces=E2=80=
=9D. The address to give applications is <code>http://localhost:4000</code>=
.</p>
    <div class=3D"note trap">
      <span class=3D"tag">Don=E2=80=99t expose this</span>
      <p>Binding to <code>0.0.0.0</code> means anyone who can reach your ma=
chine on port 4000 can spend our Azure quota, with no authentication in thi=
s configuration. Keep it on the loopback interface: decline any Windows Fir=
ewall prompt asking to allow <code>python.exe</code> on private or public n=
etworks, or start it with <code>--host 127.0.0.1</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s6">
  <div class=3D"step-num">06</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Smoke-test before touching the agent</h2>
    <p>Prove the proxy reaches Azure and that a row lands in the log. Do th=
is in the <em>second</em> terminal.</p>
    <pre><code>$body =3D @{
    model    =3D <span class=3D"s">"gpt-5.4"</span>
    messages =3D @( @{ role =3D <span class=3D"s">"user"</span>; content =
=3D <span class=3D"s">"Say hello in one sentence."</span> } )
} | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10

Invoke-RestMethod `
    -Uri <span class=3D"s">"http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions"</spa=
n> `
    -Method Post `
    -ContentType <span class=3D"s">"application/json"</span> `
    -Headers @{ <span class=3D"s">"Authorization"</span> =3D <span class=3D=
"s">"Bearer sk-local"</span> } `
    -Body $body | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10</code></pre>
    <p>Codex talks the Responses API, so test that route too =E2=80=94 it e=
xercises different code and different usage field names:</p>
    <pre><code>$body =3D @{
    model =3D <span class=3D"s">"gpt-5.4"</span>
    input =3D <span class=3D"s">"Say hello in one sentence."</span>
} | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10

Invoke-RestMethod `
    -Uri <span class=3D"s">"http://localhost:4000/v1/responses"</span> `
    -Method Post `
    -ContentType <span class=3D"s">"application/json"</span> `
    -Headers @{ <span class=3D"s">"Authorization"</span> =3D <span class=3D=
"s">"Bearer sk-local"</span> } `
    -Body $body | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10</code></pre>
    <div class=3D"note check">
      <span class=3D"tag">Confirm before moving on</span>
      <p><code>Get-Content .\token_usage.jsonl -Tail 2</code> should show t=
wo rows with non-zero <code>input_tokens</code> and <code>output_tokens</co=
de>. If the file doesn=E2=80=99t exist, the callback never fired =E2=80=94 =
go back to step 05.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s7">
  <div class=3D"step-num">07</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Point Codex CLI at the proxy</h2>
    <p>Codex reads <code>%USERPROFILE%\.codex\config.toml</code>. Add a cus=
tom provider and select it:</p>
    <span class=3D"filename">%USERPROFILE%\.codex\config.toml</span>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">model</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"gpt-5.=
4"</span>
<span class=3D"k">model_provider</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"litellm"</sp=
an>

[<span class=3D"k">model_providers.litellm</span>]
<span class=3D"k">name</span>     =3D <span class=3D"s">"LiteLLM (local)"</=
span>
<span class=3D"k">base_url</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"http://localhost:4=
000/v1"</span>
<span class=3D"k">env_key</span>  =3D <span class=3D"s">"LITELLM_API_KEY"</=
span>
<span class=3D"k">wire_api</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"responses"</span><=
/code></pre>
    <p>Then, in the terminal you run Codex from:</p>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">$env:LITELLM_API_KEY</span> =3D <span clas=
s=3D"s">"sk-local"</span>
codex</code></pre>
    <div class=3D"note">
      <span class=3D"tag">About that key</span>
      <p>The proxy has no master key configured, so it accepts anything =E2=
=80=94 but Codex refuses to start if the variable named by <code>env_key</c=
ode> is empty. Any non-empty string works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"note trap">
      <span class=3D"tag">If Codex errors on the wire protocol</span>
      <p>Recent Codex versions expect <code>wire_api =3D "responses"</code>=
 for third-party providers. Older builds may only speak <code>"chat"</code>=
. If you see protocol or 404 errors on <code>/v1/responses</code>, flip it =
to <code>wire_api =3D "chat"</code> =E2=80=94 the logger reads either shape=
, so your measurements stay valid. Check with <code>codex --version</code> =
and note which mode you ended up in, since it changes what the agent sends.=
</p>
    </div>
    <h3>If you also want to try Cursor</h3>
    <p>Cursor accepts a custom OpenAI base URL under Settings =E2=86=92 Mod=
els =E2=80=94 use <code>http://localhost:4000/v1</code> and any API key. Be=
 aware it=E2=80=99s a weaker measurement: not all of Cursor=E2=80=99s traff=
ic honours the override, so the log will undercount. Treat Codex CLI as the=
 reference and Cursor as directional.</p>
    <div class=3D"note check">
      <span class=3D"tag">Confirm before moving on</span>
      <p>Ask the agent something trivial, then check the proxy window shows=
 the request and the JSONL file grew. Once you see agent traffic in the log=
, the plumbing is done.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s8">
  <div class=3D"step-num">08</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Label runs so the totals mean something</h2>
    <p>Every row needs to say which task it belongs to, or the log is just =
an undifferentiated pile. Two ways, in order of preference:</p>
    <h3>Environment variable =E2=80=94 always works</h3>
    <p>Set a label, restart the proxy, run one task, stop. It=E2=80=99s cru=
de but it never fails:</p>
    <pre><code><span class=3D"k">$env:RUN_LABEL</span> =3D <span class=3D"s=
">"codex--refactor-auth--run1"</span>
litellm --config .\config.yaml --port 4000</code></pre>
    <p>Use a consistent naming scheme =E2=80=94 <code>&lt;agent&gt;--&lt;ta=
sk&gt;--&lt;run&gt;</code> makes the rollup readable and sortable.</p>
    <h3>Request header =E2=80=94 if your agent supports it</h3>
    <p>If your Codex version supports extra headers on a provider, add them=
 and you can label without restarting:</p>
    <pre><code>[<span class=3D"k">model_providers.litellm.http_headers</spa=
n>]
<span class=3D"s">"x-run-label"</span> =3D <span class=3D"s">"codex--refact=
or-auth--run1"</span></code></pre>
    <div class=3D"note">
      <span class=3D"tag">If the label comes through as =E2=80=9Cunlabeled=
=E2=80=9D</span>
      <p>LiteLLM=E2=80=99s metadata shape shifts between versions. Add <cod=
e>print(json.dumps(meta, default=3Dstr)[:2000])</code> at the top of <code>=
_write</code>, send one request, and read where the headers actually landed=
 =E2=80=94 then fix the key. Remove the print afterwards.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s9">
  <div class=3D"step-num">09</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Roll the log up into a report</h2>
    <p>No extra dependencies =E2=80=94 PowerShell parses the JSONL directly=
:</p>
    <span class=3D"filename">rollup.ps1</span>
    <pre><code>$rows =3D Get-Content .\token_usage.jsonl | ForEach-Object {=
 $_ | ConvertFrom-Json }

$rows | Group-Object run | ForEach-Object {
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Run       =3D $_.Name
        Calls     =3D $_.Count
        Input     =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object input_tokens          -Sum=
).Sum
        Cached    =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object cached_input_tokens   -Sum=
).Sum
        Uncached  =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object uncached_input_tokens -Sum=
).Sum
        Output    =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object output_tokens         -Sum=
).Sum
        Reasoning =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object reasoning_tokens      -Sum=
).Sum
        Visible   =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object visible_output_tokens -Sum=
).Sum
        Total     =3D ($_.Group | Measure-Object total_tokens          -Sum=
).Sum
    }
} | Sort-Object Run | Format-Table -AutoSize</code></pre>
    <p>Which gives you the thing worth putting in front of people:</p>
    <div class=3D"tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Run</th><th class=3D"num">Calls</th><th class=3D"num">Inp=
ut</th><th class=3D"num">Cached</th><th class=3D"num">Uncached</th><th clas=
s=3D"num">Output</th><th class=3D"num">Reasoning</th><th class=3D"num">Tota=
l</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>codex--refactor-auth--run1</code></td><td class=3D"=
num">18</td><td class=3D"num">1,284,392</td><td class=3D"num">943,104</td><=
td class=3D"num">341,288</td><td class=3D"num">74,821</td><td class=3D"num"=
>51,440</td><td class=3D"num">1,359,213</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>codex--refactor-auth--run2</code></td><td class=3D"=
num">16</td><td class=3D"num">1,141,006</td><td class=3D"num">988,220</td><=
td class=3D"num">152,786</td><td class=3D"num">68,004</td><td class=3D"num"=
>46,110</td><td class=3D"num">1,209,010</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class=3D"caption">Illustrative shape, not real measurements. Note ho=
w much the second run benefits from a warm cache =E2=80=94 which is exactly=
 the trap in the next step.</p>
    <p>Add <code>| Export-Csv .\rollup.csv -NoTypeInformation</code> to the=
 end of the pipeline when you want it in a spreadsheet.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"s10">
  <div class=3D"step-num">10</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Run a comparison that=E2=80=99s actually fair</h2>
    <p>The instrumentation is the easy half. Most agent comparisons go wron=
g in the experiment design, and the numbers look authoritative either way. =
Hold these constant:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Repository state.</strong> Same commit for every run, and=
 reset it between runs =E2=80=94 <code>git stash</code> or a fresh clone. A=
n agent that runs second against a half-fixed repo has an easier job.</li>
      <li><strong>Task text.</strong> Byte-identical prompt for each agent.=
 Paste from a file rather than retyping.</li>
      <li><strong>Reasoning effort.</strong> If agents default to different=
 <code>reasoning_effort</code> levels, you=E2=80=99re measuring the setting=
, not the agent. Pin it =E2=80=94 either in each agent=E2=80=99s config, or=
 centrally in <code>config.yaml</code> under <code>litellm_params</code>.</=
li>
      <li><strong>Cache state.</strong> The big one. Azure=E2=80=99s prompt=
 cache means a repeat run of the same task can post dramatically lower unca=
ched input. Either run each agent cold-first in alternating order, or run e=
verything twice and report cold and warm separately. Never compare one agen=
t=E2=80=99s cold run to another=E2=80=99s warm run.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>And when reporting: lead with <strong>uncached input</strong>, <stro=
ng>reasoning tokens</strong>, and <strong>request count</strong>. Those thr=
ee explain almost all of the variance between agents. <code>total_tokens</c=
ode> alone hides which agent is expensive and why.</p>
    <div class=3D"note">
      <span class=3D"tag">Sanity check</span>
      <p>Spot-check one task against the Azure portal=E2=80=99s own metrics=
 for the deployment. They won=E2=80=99t match to the token, but they should=
 be within a few percent. A large gap means traffic is bypassing the proxy =
=E2=80=94 usually a second agent config still pointed at Azure directly.</p=
>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"trouble">
  <div class=3D"step-num">REF</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <div class=3D"tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause and fix</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Rows land in the log but all token counts are <code>0</code=
></td>
            <td>Streaming response with no usage attached. On Chat Completi=
ons the client must send <code>stream_options: {"include_usage": true}</cod=
e>; you can force it centrally with <code>stream_options: {"include_usage":=
 true}</code> under <code>litellm_params</code>. On the Responses API, usag=
e rides on the final event =E2=80=94 if it=E2=80=99s missing, LiteLLM isn=
=E2=80=99t aggregating the stream.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>reasoning_tokens</code> always 0, everything else pop=
ulated</td>
            <td>Either the API version predates reasoning-usage reporting, =
or the deployment isn=E2=80=99t a reasoning model. Check <code>AZURE_API_VE=
RSION</code> first.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>DeploymentNotFound</code> / 404 from Azure</td>
            <td><code>litellm_params.model</code> is set to the model name =
instead of the deployment name. It must be <code>azure/&lt;deployment&gt;</=
code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>Unsupported parameter: max_tokens</code></td>
            <td>GPT-5 series wants <code>max_completion_tokens</code>. Set =
<code>drop_params: true</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>tool_choice</code> or <code>reasoning_effort</code> r=
ejected</td>
            <td>LiteLLM didn=E2=80=99t recognise the deployment as GPT-5.4.=
 Confirm <code>model_info.base_model</code>, upgrade LiteLLM, or rename the=
 deployment so it contains <code>gpt-5.4</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Codex exits immediately with a 401</td>
            <td><code>$env:LITELLM_API_KEY</code> is unset in that terminal=
. Codex requires the variable named by <code>env_key</code> to be non-empty=
.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No <code>token_usage.jsonl</code> at all</td>
            <td>The callback didn=E2=80=99t import. Start <code>litellm</co=
de> from the folder containing <code>usage_logger.py</code>, and check the =
<code>callbacks:</code> path spells the module and instance exactly.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>litellm : The term ... is not recognized</code></td>
            <td>Virtual environment not activated in this terminal.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class=3D"step" id=3D"later">
  <div class=3D"step-num">NEXT</div>
  <div class=3D"step-body">
    <h2>If we outgrow the JSONL file</h2>
    <p>LiteLLM ships an admin UI at <code>http://localhost:4000/ui</code> w=
ith spend tracking, virtual keys, and per-key reporting =E2=80=94 but it=E2=
=80=99s backed by a database. Without a Postgres connection configured, the=
re=E2=80=99s no persistent spend history to show, which is why this runbook=
 doesn=E2=80=99t rely on it.</p>
    <p>For a single-machine comparison the flat file is genuinely better: i=
t=E2=80=99s greppable, diffable, and trivially shared. Move to the database=
 setup when we want multiple people=E2=80=99s usage in one place, per-devel=
oper keys, or cost figures rather than raw token counts.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <p>Questions or a step that didn=E2=80=99t work as written =E2=80=94 send=
 the proxy window=E2=80=99s output and the last few lines of <code>token_us=
age.jsonl</code>, that=E2=80=99s usually enough to spot it.</p>
</footer>

</div>

</body></html>
